---
import { SITE } from '../lib/site.mjs';

// The analytics notice. Markup only: public/consent.js owns the behaviour and
// public/analytics.js owns the tag and window.dbhqAnalytics, both external
// because this site's CSP has no 'unsafe-inline' in script-src.
//
// It informs, it does not ask. Analytics runs by default under the PECR
// statistical-purposes exception, which needs clear information and a simple,
// free way to object rather than prior consent. It replaced a modal
// Accept/Decline dialog on 1 Oct 2026, and the pattern is the same on every
// dbhq.uk site.
//
// Non-modal: a fixed panel at the foot of the page, no backdrop and no focus
// trap, because there is nothing to agree to before reading. It ships hidden
// and consent.js shows it only when no choice is stored, or when "Cookie
// settings" in the footer reopens it.
//
// OK and Opt out are the same size and sit side by side with Opt out first.
// Objecting must be no harder than carrying on. The lime is not used here: it
// is rationed to one fill per page and a cookie notice should not spend it.
//
// This is a frontmatter comment on purpose: an HTML comment would be served to
// every reader.
---

<aside class="analytics-notice" data-analytics-notice aria-label="Analytics notice" tabindex="-1" hidden>
  <p class="analytics-notice-text">
    This site counts visits with Google Analytics to see which pages are useful. No advertising, and nothing shared with advertisers. <a href={`${SITE.privacy}#analytics`}>How it works</a>
  </p>
  <p class="analytics-notice-status" data-analytics-status hidden></p>
  <div class="analytics-notice-actions">
    <button type="button" class="btn ghost" data-analytics-off>Opt out</button>
    <button type="button" class="btn" data-analytics-on>OK</button>
  </div>
</aside>
